drm/panel: pick the Gemini's panel at runtime, and stop naming the driver
 after the wrong one

The Gemini shipped with two panels on identical wiring — a Novatek NT36672
and a Solomon SSD2092 — so the device tree cannot say which is fitted, and
a build that assumes one gives a dark screen on the other. Until now this
driver assumed, twice: first that it was an SSD2092, and then, when that
turned out to be wrong, by being retargeted in place to the NT36672 and
throwing the SSD2092's transcription away.

Ask instead. LK probes the fitted panel at every boot and hands the kernel
the LCM's name in the videolfb ATAG, which arrives as
/chosen/atag,videolfb — 8 bytes of framebuffer base, an "lcm found" flag,
fps, vram, then a NUL-terminated string. On this unit it reads
"aeon_nt36672_fhd_dsi_vdo_x600_xinli". That is the same identity the
vendor kernel selects its LCM driver by.

So: a variant table keyed on that string, carrying each panel's mode, DSI
mode flags (the NT36672 is BURST, the SSD2092 SYNC_PULSE) and init
sequence. The SSD2092's 188-command sequence is restored verbatim from
this driver's first version and marked as never having run on hardware,
because it has not — there is no SSD2092 unit in this lab. It is here so a
build serves both panels and so the transcription is not lost a second
time.

When LK says nothing recognisable, fall back to the NT36672 and say so at
warn level rather than refusing to probe. A machine that boots with a
display that might be wrong is recoverable over the network; one that
boots with no display, on a device whose only other way in is a USB cable,
is much less so. The message is what makes the difference diagnosable.

And the rename, which is the other half of the same bug. This file was
called panel-solomon-ssd2092.c and asserted the Solomon identity in its
header comment for six weeks while driving a Novatek part. That is a large
part of why B-31 survived as long as it did: the touchscreen fault was a
display fault, and every reader who came to the display looking for a
Novatek TDDI part found a file about a Solomon controller and moved on. It
is now panel-gemini-fhd.c / DRM_PANEL_GEMINI_FHD, named after the machine
rather than after either panel, with a new "planet,gemini-panel"
compatible. "solomon,ssd2092" is kept in the match table on purpose: every
DTB flashed before today carries it, and some of those live in boot slots
that exist to be fallen back to.

Provenance of the golden values and both generated init tables is carried
through the move unchanged.

Verified on hardware: "bootloader reports Novatek NT36672 (TDDI)
(aeon_nt36672_fhd_dsi_vdo_x600_xinli)", init sequence 183 commands in
156 ms — byte-identical to before — panel gate PASS 13/13, desktop
photographed on the glass.

Signed-off-by: mratan <mattdratan@gmail.com>

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-solomon-ssd2092.c
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,716 +0,0 @@
-// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
-/*
- * Solomon SSD2092 FHD DSI panel driver
- *
- * Panel: 5.99" 1080x2160 MIPI DSI video-mode (sync-pulse) panel,
- * Solomon SSD2092 controller, panel ID 0x01572098.
- * Used on: Planet Computers Gemini PDA (MT6797X / Helio X27).
- *
- * This is the panel actually fitted to the Gemini PDA: the stock LK
- * bootloader probes it at every boot ("we will use lcm:
- * aeon_ssd2092_fhd_dsi_solomon", ID read 0x01572098). Earlier project
- * documentation assumed the R63419 WQHD panel from the vendor DTB's
- * videolfb template string, but that atag is a build-time default LK
- * overwrites after probing.
- *
- * All hardware values (timings, lane count, init sequence, reset
- * sequence) come from the vendor 3.18 kernel LCM driver:
- *   drivers/misc/mediatek/lcm/aeon_ssd2092_fhd_dsi_solomon/
- *     aeon_ssd2092_fhd_dsi_solomon.c
- *   (gemini-android-kernel-3.18-android8 tree)
- * Vendor params: SYNC_PULSE_VDO_MODE, LCM_FOUR_LANE, RGB888,
- * VSA/VBP/VFP = 1/43/76, HSA/HBP/HFP = 4/20/26, PLL_CLOCK = 502 MHz.
- *
- * LCD bias: dual-output +/-5V charge pump (vendor calls it lp3101,
- * same part family/address as TPS65132), I2C, addr 0x3E. Exposed here
- * as avdd/avee regulator supplies.
- */
